2026-06-30 15:43:55,395 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-44856407-0547-4c50-b290-1067e55e9a9c', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF factor value terms. Factor values identify which metadata\nattribute is the primary experimental variable being compared in the study.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: factor value cell line\nsdrf_column: factor value[cell line]\nontology: CLO:0000031\ndescription: Indicates cell line is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return a value if different cell lines are compared across conditions.\n  Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value compound\nsdrf_column: factor value[compound]\nontology: CHEBI:37577\ndescription: Indicates compound/drug treatment is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return the compound name if different compounds or drug doses are\n  compared. Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value disease\nsdrf_column: factor value[disease]\nontology: OGMS:0000031, MONDO, EFO, DOID\ndescription: Indicates disease state is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return disease name/accession if diseased vs. normal or different\n  disease states are compared. Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value dose\nsdrf_column: factor value[dose]\nontology: EFO:0000428\ndescription: Indicates dose/concentration is the primary experimental variable.\npattern: number + unit\ninstructions: Return the dose range if different doses of a compound are compared.\n  Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value organism part\nsdrf_column: factor value[organism part]\nontology: EFO:0000635, UBERON, BTO\ndescription: Indicates tissue/organ is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return the tissue/organ name if different tissues are compared.\n  Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value time\nsdrf_column: factor value[time]\nontology: EFO:0000721\ndescription: Indicates time point is the primary experimental variable.\npattern: number + time unit\ninstructions: Return the time points compared if a time-course experiment.\n  Otherwise return null.\n\nterm: factor value treatment\nsdrf_column: factor value[treatment]\nontology: EFO:0000727, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Indicates treatment/perturbation is the primary experimental variable.\ninstructions: Return the treatment type if different treatments are compared\n  (e.g. different growth conditions, carbon sources, stress conditions).\n  Otherwise return null.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string or null — null if this variable is not\n           the primary experimental factor in this study)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Abstract", "Methods"), or null\n\nNote: Most XL-MS datasets will have only one or two non-null factor values.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': "[TITLE]\nThe proximity interactome of PML isoforms I and II under fatty acid stress\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nPromyelocytic leukemia (PML) protein forms the scaffold for PML nuclear bodies (PML NB) that reorganize into Lipid‐Associated PML Structures (LAPS) under fatty acid stress. We determined how the fatty acid oleate alters the interactome of PMLI or PMLII by expressing fusions with the ascorbate peroxidase APEX2 in U2OS cells. The resultant interactome included ESCRT and COPII transport protein nodes. Proximity ligation assay (PLA) revealed that COPII proteins SEC23B, SEC24A and USO1 preferentially associated with PML NBs. Nuclear localization of USO1, but not SEC23B and SEC24A, was reduced in PML knockout cells and restored by PMLII expression. Thus, proximity‐labelling methods identified COPII transport protein interactions with PML NBs that are disrupted by fatty acid stress.\nAPEX2 proximity labelling and proximity ligations assays revealed the protein interactome of PML in nuclear bodies, and how these interactions are modified when PML associates with nuclear lipid droplets under conditions of fatty acid stress. A novel interaction node including COPII transport proteins SEC23B, SAC24A and U2OS preferentially associated with PML in nuclear bodies rather than on nuclear lipid droplets.\n\n[METHODS]\nMaterials and methods\nCell culture and transfection\nHuman osteosarcoma U2OS cells (RRID:CVCL_0042) were purchased from and authenticated by ATCC (Cat# HTB‐96). U2OS were used to derive U2OS PML knockout and U2OS Clover‐PML knock‐in cells, cells were mycoplasma‐free (based on PCR testing) and cultured in DMEM containing 10% FBS at 37\u2009°C in a 5% CO2 atmosphere. To induce the formation of TAG‐rich lipid droplets, cells were incubated with an oleate/BSA complex (6.6/1, mol:mol) at the concentrations indicated in figure legends. Cells were transfected with plasmids using Lipofectamine 2000 at a DNA (μg)\u2009:\u2009Lipofectamine (μL) ratio of 1\u2009:\u20093 for 48\u2009h prior to the start of experiments.\nConstruction of APEX2 expression vectors\npAPEX2‐HA‐PMLI and ‐PMLII were constructed by PCR amplification of the APEX2‐HA fragment from pUC57‐APEX2‐HA using forward and reverse primers containing Nhe1 and Bsrg1 restriction sites, respectively. The APEX2‐HA fragment was ligated into pEGFP‐C1‐PMLI and pEGFP‐C1‐PMLII that were digested with the same enzymes to remove the GFP cassette. pAPEX2‐HA‐NLS was constructed by PCR amplification of APEX2‐HA fragment from pUC57‐APEX2‐HA using forward and reverse primers containing Kpn1 and Bsrg1 restriction sites, respectively. The APEX2‐HA fragment was cloned into pEGFP‐NLS‐N1 (3 NLS cassettes inserted at the Bsrg1 site) that was digested with Kpn1 and Bsrg1 to remove the GFP cassette.\nBiotin‐phenol labelling of cells expressing APEX2 fusion proteins\nU2OS cells transiently transfected with pAPEX2‐HA‐PML‐I, pAPEX2‐HA‐PML‐II, or pAPEX2‐HA‐NLS were treated with or without 500\u2009μm oleate for 24\u2009h. Cells were then incubated with 0.5\u2009mm biotin‐phenol for 30\u2009min at 37\u2009°C in a 5% CO2 incubator, 1\u2009mm hydrogen peroxide was added for 1\u2009min at room temperature, and the reaction terminated by the addition of 0.5\u2009mL of quench solution (10\u2009mm Trolox, 20\u2009mm ascorbate in PBS, pH 7). Cells were rinsed twice with 0.5\u2009mL of quench solution and either lysed for SDS/PAGE, fixed for immunofluorescence or isolated on NeutrAvidin‐agarose (Thermo‐Fisher Scientific, Burlington, ON, Canada) for mass spectrometry of biotinylated peptides (see below).\nIsolation of biotinylated proteins on NeutrAvidin‐agarose\nAfter proximity biotin labelling as described above, cells were scraped in PBS, collected by centrifugation at 3000\u2009 g  for 5\u2009min and the cell pellet was dissolved for 5\u2009min on ice in 1\u2009mL of 50\u2009mm Tris (pH 7.4), 5\u2009mm Trolox, 0.5% (w/v) sodium deoxycholate, 150\u2009mm NaCl, 0.1% (w/v) SDS, 1% (v/v) Triton X‐100, 1\u2009mm phenylmethanesulfonylfluoride, 10\u2009mm NaN3, 10\u2009mm sodium ascorbate, and protease inhibitor cocktail. Cell lysates were centrifuged for 10\u2009min at 13\u2009000\u2009 g  at 4\u2009°C and the supernatant collected and incubated with NeutrAvidin‐agarose for 12\u2009h at 4\u2009°C on an orbital rotor. The beads were collected by brief centrifugation, washed once with buffer 1 (50\u2009mm HEPES, pH 7.4, 0.1% (w/v) sodium deoxycholate, 1% (v/v) Triton X‐100, 500\u2009mm NaCl, and 1\u2009mm EDTA), once with buffer 2 (50\u2009mm Tris, pH 8.0, 250\u2009mm LiCl, 0.5% (v/v) Nonidet P40, 0.5% (w/v) sodium deoxycholate, and 1\u2009mm EDTA), and twice with buffer 3 (50\u2009mm Tris, pH 7.4, 50\u2009mm NaCl). During each step, the beads were incubated for 5\u2009min at 4\u2009°C on an orbital rotor. The proteins were eluted from the agarose beads by incubation with SDS lysis buffer supplemented with 5\u2009mm desthiobiotin for 5\u2009min at 95\u2009°C. Protein samples were then resolved by SDS/PAGE and immunoblotted to detect biotinylated proteins (see below).\nIdentification of biotinylated proteins by quantitative mass spectrometry\nSample preparation and analysis was conducted as previously described. Proteins bound to NeutrAvidin‐agarose were subject to five washes with 20\u2009mm NH4CHO3, reduced with 10\u2009mm DTT for 30\u2009min and alkylated with 15\u2009mm chloroacetamide for 1\u2009h. On‐bead digestion with trypsin (1\u2009μg overnight) was stopped with 1% formic acid. Eluted peptides were collected, and the beads were treated with 1% formic acid and 60% acetonitrile to release residual peptides. Pooled eluates were dried, resuspended in 0.1% trifluoroacetate (TFA) and peptides were desalted using 100\u2009μL pipet tips containing a C18 resin (Thermo‐Fisher Scientific, Burlington, ON, Canada). Peptides were dried, resuspended in 1% formic acid and 500\u2009ng was injected into an HPLC (nanoElute, Bruker Daltonics, Burlington, ON, Canada) equipped with a trap column (Acclaim PepMap100 C18, 0.3\u2009mm id\u2009×\u20095\u2009mm, Dionex Corporation, Sunnyvale, CA, USA) and analytical C18 column (1.9\u2009μm beads, 75\u2009μm\u2009×\u200925\u2009cm, PepSep, Bruker Daltonics, Burlington, ON, Canada). Peptides were separated over 2\u2009h using a linear gradient of 5–37% acetonitrile in 0.1% TFA at a flow rate of 400\u2009nL·min−1 while being injected into a TimsTOF Pro ion mobility mass spectrometer equipped with a Captive Spray nano electrospray source (Bruker Daltonics). The target intensity was set to 20\u2009000, with an intensity threshold of 2500. Samples processing was acquired using diaPASEF mode. Briefly, for each single TIMS (100\u2009ms) in diaPASEF mode, we used 1 mobility window consisting of 27 mass steps (m/z between 114 to 1414 with a mass width of 50\u2009Da) per cycle (1.27\u2009s duty cycle) and collision energy of 42.0\u2009eV. These steps cover the diagonal scan line for +2 and +3 charged peptides in the m/z‐ion mobility plane.\nThe raw files were analyzed using dia‐nn software (version 1.8.1) and the Uniprot human proteome database (21/03/2020, 75\u2009776 entries). FASTA digest for library‐free search/library generation was enabled, as well as Deep learning‐based spectra, retention time and ion mobility prediction. The settings used for the analysis were: 1 miscleavage was allowed; fixed modifications were carbamidomethylation on cysteine and N‐terminal methionine excision; enzyme was trypsin; peptide length between 7 and 30 amino acids; precursor charge range was between 2 and 4; a mass tolerance of 20\u2009ppm was fixed for both precursor ions and fragment ions; precursor false discovery rate was set to 5%; match between runs, unrelated runs and MaxLFQ options were also enabled.\nThe average peptide count data was filtered using the CRAPome repository with a threshold score of 0.2. to remove common mass spectrometry contaminants. The residual proteins from the APEX2‐PMLI and ‐PMLII data sets were then compared against the APEX2‐NLS protein data sets using the default settings of the program SAINTexpress using a score threshold of 0.2. Dot plots of the resulting proteins were made using ProHits‐viz with a Bayesian false discovery rate threshold of 0.05 and interaction networks analyzed using string v11.\nImmunoblotting\nCells were rinsed and scraped in cold PBS, collected by centrifugation at 10\u2009000\u2009 g  for 10\u2009min at 4\u2009°C, and the cell pellet lysed with SDS lysis buffer (62.5\u2009mm Tris/HCl, pH 6.8, 10% glycerol, 2% SDS, 0.05% bromophenol blue and 5% β‐mercaptoethanol) on ice for 10\u2009min. Cell lysates were sonicated for 10\u2009s, denatured at 90\u2009°C for 3\u2009min, resolved by SDS/PAGE and transferred to nitrocellulose filters. After incubation in LI‐COR Odyssey blocking solution diluted 4\u2009:\u20091 (v/v) in TBS‐Tween (20\u2009mm Tris/HCl, pH 7.4, 150\u2009mm NaCl, 0.1% Tween 20) for 1\u2009h at 20\u2009°C, the filters were incubated with the following primary antibodies; HA mouse monoclonal (Cell Signalling, Whitby, ON, Canada, Cat# 2367S), PML rabbit poly clonal (Bethyl Laboratories, Montgomery, TX, USA Cat# A301‐167A), SEC24A rabbit polyclonal (Sigma Aldrich, Oakville, ON, Canada, Cat# HPA056825), SEC23B rabbit polyclonal (Sigma Aldrich, Oakville, ON, Canada, Cat# HPA008216), or USO1 rabbit polyclonal (Proteintech, Rosemont, IL, USA, Cat# 13509‐1‐AP). After rinsing in TBS‐Tween, the filters were incubated with goat anti‐mouse and goat anti‐rabbit secondary antibodies conjugated to LICOR680 or 800 IRDye, or streptavidin‐conjugated LICOR680 IRDye (LICORbio, Lincoln, NE, USA) for 1\u2009h at 20\u2009°C. Nitrocellulose membranes were imaged and fluorescence quantified using a LICOR Odyssey fluorescence scanner and associated software (v3.0).\nImmunofluorescence confocal microscopy\nCells cultured on glass coverslips (0.13–0.16\u2009mm) were fixed with paraformaldehyde (4%, w/v) in PBS for 15\u2009min at 20\u2009°C, permeabilized with Triton X‐100 (0.1%, v/v) in PBS for 10\u2009min at 4\u2009°C and blocked with BSA (1%, w/v) in PBS (PBS/BSA) for 1\u2009h at 20\u2009°C. For immunostaining with SEC23B and SEC24A antibodies, cells were fixed in 100% methanol at −20\u2009°C for 2\u2009min and blocked with PBS/BSA. Coverslips were incubated with the following primary antibodies diluted in PBS/BSA overnight at 4\u2009°C; FLAG mouse monoclonal (Sigma Aldrich, Cat# F1804), PML mouse monoclonal (Santa Cruz Biotechnology, Dallas, TX, USA, Cat# sc‐377390) as well as those listed in the previous section. After washing in PBS/BSA, coverslips were incubated with goat anti‐rabbit and goat anti‐mouse secondary antibodies conjugated to AlexaFluor‐488, ‐594 or ‐647 or streptavidin‐AlexaFluor‐488 (Thermo‐Fisher). LDs were detected with BODIPY493/503, LipidTOX neutral red or LipidTOX deep red (Thermo‐Fisher) diluted in PBS. Nuclei were visualized with Hoechst. Coverslips were mounted in Mowiol on glass slides and imaged using a Leica SP8 LIGHTNING confocal microscope with a 63× oil immersion HC Plan APOCHROMAT objective, 405, 488, 552 and 638\u2009nm lasers, and leica las x software. For 3D renderings, three‐channel confocal Z‐stacks of USO1 Clover‐PML cells immunostained for USO1, LipidTOX neutral red and Hoechst were captured using a Leica SP8 LIGHTNING. Z‐stacks were rendered using imaris software (9.7.0) and regions of interest were cropped as previously described.\nTo quantify USO1 nuclear area, images were converted to 8‐bit and a tailored threshold applied for the Hoechst channel using image j (v1.53). Total nuclear area using the Hoechst channel was quantified using the Measure function. To quantify USO1 nuclear area, a nuclear mask was applied to the USO1 channel, nuclear USO1 was converted to 8‐bit, a uniform threshold applied, and the area was quantified using the Measure function. Nuclear USO1 was expressed as a fraction of total nuclear area.\nProximity ligation assay\nAfter treatments, U2OS and PML KO cells seeded on coverslips (0.13–0.16\u2009mm) were fixed and permeabilized as described in the previous section. Cells were incubated with primary mouse/rabbit antibody pairs overnight at 4\u2009°C in PBS/BSA: CCTα rabbit polyclonal, DAXX rabbit polyclonal (Sigma Aldrich, Cat# D7810) as well as those listed in the previous sections. PLA were performed according to the manufacturer's instructions (DuoLink Red kit, Sigma Aldrich). After removing primary antibodies, PLA anti‐rabbit PLUS and anti‐mouse MINUS secondary antibodies (diluted in 40\u2009μL PBS/BSA) were spotted on parafilm in a humidified chamber, and coverslips were inverted on the droplets for 1\u2009h at 37\u2009°C. After removing the secondary antibodies, ligation and amplification reactions using fluorophore‐labeled oligonucleotides (λEx593 nm/λEx 622) were performed in 40\u2009μL reaction mixtures at 37\u2009°C for 30 and 100\u2009min, respectively. To detect the primary antigens, coverslips were incubated with anti‐rabbit or anti‐mouse secondary antibodies conjugated to AlexaFluor‐488 or ‐647. Coverslips were dried before being mounted on glass slides using In Situ mounting medium containing Hoechst and sealed with nail polish. To quantify PLA puncta, image j (v1.53) was used to convert each PLA and PML images to 8‐bit and a uniform threshold applied to each channel. The number of PML positive puncta was quantified from these two channels using the AND Image Calculator and Analyze Particles function with the default settings. For the quantification of PLA puncta, the Analyze Particles function was used on the PLA channel alone. The number of nuclei were quantified from the Hoechst channel.\n\n[RESULTS]\nResults\n APEX2‐PMLI and APEX2‐PMLII localize to PML NBs and LAPS \nThe interactome of PML NBs and LAPS was investigated by biotin proximity labeling of these structures in U2OS cells using APEX2 fused to the N‐terminus of PMLI and PMLII (referred to hereafter as APEX2‐PMLI and APEX2‐PMLII, Fig.\xa01A). We chose APEX2 in part due to its smaller mass compared to biotin ligases, rapid catalysis for better temporal resolution of bait‐protein interactions, and synthesis of a short‐lived biotin‐phenol radical that reacts with electron‐rich tyrosine residues. Biotin ligases that modify lysine residues were not used for this application since PML and many of its client proteins are frequently modified by SUMOylation of this amino acid. To control for non‐specific nuclear biotinylation, APEX2 with a C‐terminal NLS (referred to hereafter as APEX2‐NLS) was expressed in cells. All three constructs have an internal HA tag to visualize expression by confocal microscopy and immunoblotting in transiently transfected U2OS cells, which are ideal for this study because they express abundant PML NBs and LAPS. APEX2‐PMLI, and APEX2‐PML‐II and APEX2‐NLS were expressed in U2OS cells at the expected molecular mass of 125\u2009kDa when probed with an HA antibody (Fig.\xa01B). Confocal immunofluorescence was used to determine if APEX2‐PMLI and ‐PMLII associated with PML NBs and LAPS (Fig.\xa01C,D). In transiently transfected U2OS cells both APEX2‐PMLI and APEX2‐PMLII were localized to nuclear puncta corresponding to PML NBs that were positive for HA and PML (Fig.\xa01C,D). Treatment of these cells with oleate for 24\u2009h resulted in the appearance of BODIPY493/503‐positive nLDs that had the HA‐tagged fusion proteins on their surface (Fig.\xa01C,D) indicating that both APEX2‐PML probes were incorporated into LAPS. The nucleus also contains a pool of soluble, non‐SUMOylated PML that can assemble into PML NBs under stress conditions. HA and PML diffusely stained the nucleus in control and oleate‐treated cells indicating that soluble APEX2‐PML1 and II interactions will be detected (Fig.\xa01C,D).\nNext, we determined whether APEX2‐PML proteins facilitated the biotin labeling of PML NBs and LAPS. U2OS cells treated with oleate and transiently expressing APEX2‐PMLI or ‐PMLII were treated with biotin‐phenol followed by H2O2, and biotinylated proteins were visualized by immunofluorescence with streptavidin‐AlexaFluor‐488 (Fig.\xa02A). Streptavidin fluorescence was primarily restricted to the nuclei where it was concentrated on LipoTox Red‐positive nLDs. There was also biotin labelling of punctate structures that lacked LipoTox Red corresponding to PML NBs. Biotin labelling of PML NBs was also evident in cells not treated with oleate (results not shown). There was also diffuse streptavidin staining of the nucleoplasm indicating the soluble APEX2‐PMLI and II was labelling proteins not associated with PML NBs or LAPs. The APEX2‐NLS control protein was diffusely localized to the nucleoplasm and concentrated in amorphous structures that did not correspond LAPS or PML NBs (Fig.\xa02B). Lysates of U2OS cells expressing the three APEX2‐fusion proteins and cultured in the absence or presence of oleate were positive for biotinylated proteins detected by SDS/PAGE and a streptavidin‐conjugated probe (Fig.\xa02C).\nThe APEX2‐PML interactome of PML NBs and LAPS \nHaving established that APEX2‐PML proteins localized to nuclear bodies and LAPS similarly to their endogenous counterparts, APEX2‐based proximity biotinylation was used to establish the PMLI and II interactomes in control and oleate‐treated cells. U2OS cells expressing APEX2‐PMLI, APEX2‐PMLII, or APEX2‐NLS were treated with or without 500\u2009μm oleate for 24\u2009h, followed by 30 and 1\u2009min incubations with biotin‐phenol and H2O2, respectively. Prior to quantitative mass spectrometry analysis, the expression of fusion proteins and efficiency of the biotinylation reaction was confirmed in cell lysates by immunoblotting with a HA antibody and a streptavidin probe (see Fig.\xa02C). High confidence APEX2‐PMLI and ‐PMLII interactors were identified using SAINT score analysis and filtered for non‐specific interactions against the APEX2‐NLS and CRAPome data sets. A total of 64 proteins interacted with both PML isoforms in control and oleate‐treated cells and were sorted into a dot plot to illustrate the isoform‐ and condition‐specific interactions (Fig.\xa03A). Although many interacting proteins were shared between the two PML isoforms, more high confidence interactions were identified for APEX2‐PMLI. Approximately 75% of the interactors are nominally cytoplasmic or localized to cytoplasmic organelles, with the remainder designated as nuclear‐localized (Fig.\xa03B). Well‐known PML NB and LAPS interactors identified by other methodologies, such as co‐immunofluorescence, were not represented in our set suggesting that biotin proximity labelling targets a unique subset of interactions in these PML structures; a result we speculate may arise if biotinylation of some interacting proteins is occluded by post translational modifications or association with the PML structures themselves. To facilitate identification of common networks, proteins that interacted with both PMLI and PMLII in the presence (Fig.\xa03B) and absence of oleate (Fig.\xa03C) were pooled and analyzed using STRING (disconnected proteins were excluded from the network based on confidence using all active interaction sources). The resulting networks highlighted two interconnected clusters: (a) Endosomal sorting complexes required for transport (ESCRT) proteins such as TSG101, STAM and STAM2 and (b) COPII vesicular coat proteins and tethers such as SEC23B, SEC24A, SEC24B, SEC23IP and USO1. These interaction networks were evident with both PMLI and PMLII and under control and oleate‐treated conditions but were more robust for APEX2‐PMLI in oleate‐treated cells.\nInteraction of COPII transport proteins with PML NBs and LAPS \nAn intriguing feature of the PML interactome was the association of ESCRT and COPII transport proteins with PMLI and II, and the enrichment of some interactors (i.e., SEC24B, USO1, TSG101) in oleate‐treated cells (Fig.\xa03). The reported nuclear localization and functions of TSG101, SEC23B and USO1 prompted a detailed validation of PML‐specific interactions. We chose to analyze the COPII network by assessing the localization of SEC23B, SEC24A and USO1 with PML NBs and LAPS using a combination of confocal immunofluorescence and PLA.\nConsistent with their role in COPII vesicle transport between the ER and Golgi apparatus, SEC23B and SEC24A were primarily localized to the perinuclear compartment of control and oleate‐treated cells (Fig.\xa04A,B). However, SEC23B‐ and SEC24A‐positive puncta were also evident in the nuclei of cells cultured with or without oleate. Magnified ROIs showed partial co‐localization of SEC23B and SEC24A with PML NBs and LAPS as well as nLDs devoid of PML. Due to the resolution limits of confocal microscopy, the interaction of SEC23B and SEC24A with PML was evaluated by PLA. This technique involves primary antibody pairs to suspected interacting proteins, secondary antibodies with DNA tags that ligate when targets are within 40\u2009nm, and a rolling circle DNA amplification step to enhance sensitivity. Using this technique, we validated the method using proteins known to interact with PML NBs and LAPS. First, a polyclonal antibody against DAXX, a canonical PML NB‐associated protein that is depleted from LAPS, when combined with a PML monoclonal antibody produced numerous PLA puncta in nuclei of U2OS cells (Fig.\xa05A) that were strongly reduced by oleate treatment for 24\u2009h (Fig.\xa05B,C). Secondly, a polyclonal antibody against CCTα, which associates with nLDs and LAPS in oleate‐treated U2OS cells, was used in combination with a PML antibody. Unexpectedly, the combination of CCTα and PML antibodies produced numerous PLA puncta in untreated U2OS cells (Fig.\xa05D), indicating that CCTα also associates with PML NBs. However, oleate treatment caused the appearance of intense PLA signals on LAPS but an overall reduction in puncta due to the coalescence of PML and CCTα onto LAPS (Fig.\xa05E,F). PML KO U2OS cells exhibited a near complete absence of CCTα‐PML puncta confirming the specificity of PLA to detect PML‐specific interactions (Fig.\xa05F).\nWhen PLA was used to assess the SEC23B and PML interaction, antibodies against the two proteins produced discrete PLA nuclear puncta in U2OS cells that overlapped with PML NBs (Fig.\xa06A). Quantification of puncta showed that oleate treatment diminished the SEC23B‐PML interaction but it was still significantly greater than PML KO cells exposed to fatty acids (Fig.\xa06B). Similarly, PLA puncta using SEC24A and PML antibodies in U2OS cells were reduced 4‐fold by oleate treatment and almost completely absent in PML KO cells with or without fatty acid stress (Fig.\xa06C,D). Collectively, the reduced SEC23B and SEC24A interaction with PML following oleate mirrored that of DAXX suggesting the two COPII coat proteins preferentially interact with PML NBs rather than LAPS. The number of PLA interactions detected with SEC23B, SEC24A and PML antibodies (2–2.5 per cell) was lower than with DAXX (15 per cell) indicating fewer SEC23B and SEC24A interactions with PML or that methanol fixation used for PLA partially disrupted PML NBs.\nUSO1 is a coiled‐coil protein that has a canonical role in the tethering of COPII vesicles in the ER‐Golgi secretory pathway. Immunofluorescence confocal microscopy of control and oleate‐treated U2OS cells revealed the expected localization of USO1 to the perinuclear Golgi apparatus as well as USO1‐positive structures that partially localized to PML NBs, LAPS and nLDs (Fig.\xa07A). Confocal sections through the nuclei of U2OS Clover‐PML cells showed the presence of small USO1 structures on the periphery of large LAPS (Fig.\xa07B). 3D reconstructions of a LAPS in Fig.\xa07B confirmed that USO1 structures were on the exterior of LAPS in close proximity to PML. The proximity of USO1 to PML structures was confirmed by PLA using a USO1 and PML antibody pair that produced numerous puncta in U2OS cells that co‐localized with PML immunostaining (Fig.\xa07C). Analysis of these images (i.e., Fig.\xa07C) indicated that oleate reduced PLA puncta by 2‐fold, and that the signal was strongly attenuated in PML KO cells (Fig.\xa07D). Similar to results with SEC23B and SEC24A, USO1 appeared to preferentially interact with PML NBs.\nLastly, we investigated whether the expression and/or nuclear localization of SEC23B, SEC24A or USO1 was dependent on the expression of PML in U2OS cells. Immunoblot analysis showed that expression of the three proteins was unaffected by PML KO or treatment with oleate (Fig.\xa0S1A). As well, the nuclear localization of SEC23B and SEC24A in U2OS and PML KO cells was similar (Fig.\xa0S1B). However, immunofluorescence localization of USO1 in the nucleus of PML KO cells was reduced compared to controls but cytoplasmic Golgi/vesicular staining was unaffected (Fig.\xa08A,B). This result is shown more clearly by quantification of nuclear‐localized USO1 from images (i.e., Fig.\xa08B) and expression as a fraction of total nuclear area (Fig.\xa08C). While it was apparent that the localization of USO1 to the nucleus of U2OS cells was unaffected by oleate treatment, PML KO cells had a significant reduction in nuclear USO1 whether cultured in the presence or absence of oleate. To confirm that PML was required for nuclear localization of USO1, and to identify the PML isoform(s) responsible, USO1 nuclear localization was quantified in PML KO cells transiently expressing GFP‐PMLI and/or GFP‐PMLII. Compared to GFP controls or non‐transfected cells, expression of GFP‐PMLI did not increase nuclear USO1 (Fig.\xa08D,E). However, expression of GFP‐PMLII significantly increased nuclear USO1 (Fig.\xa08E) to a level similar to U2OS cells (Fig.\xa08C). Co‐expression of GFP‐PMLI and GFP‐PMLII resulted in a less significant increase in nuclear USO1 (Fig.\xa08E). Thus, the retention of USO1 in the nucleus is dependent on its interaction with PMLII.\n"}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
